## Structure is not skin: knowing which layer you are changing

The first useful decision in any theming project is to separate *structure* from *surface*. Bootstrap owns structure: the twelve-column grid, flex and gap utilities, spacing scales, breakpoint behaviour, the state machine behind a dropdown. Catppuccin owns surface: hue, saturation, lightness, and the relationships between foreground and background. The mistake we see most often is trying to solve a surface problem with structural tools — adding margin to make a low-contrast card feel less cramped, for example, when the actual problem is that the card background is only two steps away from the page background.

| Layer | Owns | Should not own | Where it lives |
|:---|:---|:---|:---|
| Structure | Grid, spacing, sizing, layout, responsive behaviour | Colour meaning | Bootstrap utilities and component classes |
| Surface | Text, background, border, accent, state colour | Column counts and gap sizes | CSS custom properties on `:root` and `[data-theme]` |
| Semantics | Which colour means danger, success, focus | Hex values | Your own aliases (`--state-danger`, `--state-focus`) |
| Behaviour | Theme persistence, system preference, transitions | Visual appearance | A small script, ideally under fifty lines |

The middle two rows are where most of our initial confusion lived. We started by overriding Bootstrap's `--bs-primary` directly with a Catppuccin hex value. That worked until we wanted the same colour to mean "selected" in a list and "primary action" in a form. The fix was to introduce an intermediate layer of aliases — names that describe *intent* rather than hue — and point Bootstrap's variables at those aliases.

### A mental model that survives upgrades

Bootstrap's colour system is built on [CSS custom properties](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Using_CSS_custom_properties). That is good news, because a custom property is just a name with a value attached, and names can be redirected. Our cascade looks like this:

1. Catppuccin publishes flavour values (`--ctp-mocha-mauve`, `--ctp-latte-blue`, and so on) as raw palette tokens.
2. We define intent aliases (`--state-focus`, `--surface-raised`, `--text-muted`) in terms of those raw tokens. The alias definition is the same for every flavour.
3. Bootstrap's `--bs-*` variables are assigned from the intent aliases.
4. Components consume Bootstrap's variables, exactly as they were designed to.

This ordering matters. When a new Bootstrap version renames a variable, we edit one line in step three. When Catppuccin adjusts a hue, we edit one line in step one. When a product owner decides that focus rings should be peach instead of mauve, we edit one line in step two. Three different kinds of change, three different places to make them, and no archaeology.

It also gives us a clean answer to the inevitable question, "can we just hard-code this one value?" Usually the answer is no, for a reason that has nothing to do with purity:

```css
/* Names describe intent; values describe the current flavour. */
:root {
  --state-focus: var(--ctp-mauve);
  --state-danger: var(--ctp-red);
  --state-success: var(--ctp-green);
  --surface-page: var(--ctp-base);
  --surface-raised: var(--ctp-mantle);
  --surface-sunken: var(--ctp-crust);
  --text-primary: var(--ctp-text);
  --text-muted: var(--ctp-subtext1);
  --border-subtle: var(--ctp-surface2);
}
```

The build step that generates the `--ctp-*` variables for each flavour is deliberately boring. There is no cleverness in it, no conditional logic, and no runtime colour maths. If you want to see what it produces for a single flavour, our task runner writes a flat file:

    export CTP_FLAVOR=mocha
    npm run tokens:generate
    npm run tokens:verify

`tokens:verify` compares the generated values against the upstream palette and fails the build on a mismatch. We added it after a dependency update quietly changed a single hex value and made our focus ring indistinguishable from a link for three weeks. Nobody reported it, which tells you something about how quietly a small colour regression travels.

