## Four flavours, one vocabulary

Catppuccin calls its palettes *flavours*: Latte, Frappé, Macchiato, and Mocha. They share the same colour names — `rosewater`, `flamingo`, `pink`, `mauve`, `red`, `maroon`, `peach`, `yellow`, `green`, `teal`, `sky`, `sapphire`, `blue`, `lavender`, plus the neutrals — but each flavour assigns different values. That shared vocabulary is the whole trick. A theme switch does not have to remap meanings; it only has to swap a set of values.

| Flavour | Character | Base | Best suited to | Watch out for |
|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|
| **Latte** | Light, warm, gently desaturated | `#eff1f5` | Documentation, daytime dashboards, print-adjacent views | Borders disappearing into `base` |
| **Frappé** | Dark, muted, low-key | `#303446` | Long reading sessions, low-light environments | Accents feeling *too* quiet for primary actions |
| **Macchiato** | Dark, medium contrast, balanced | `#24273a` | General-purpose product UI | Nothing in particular; it is the safest default |
| **Mocha** | Dark, rich, saturated | `#1e1e2e` | Marketing surfaces, code-heavy tools, night mode | Overusing saturated accents in dense tables |

![The four Catppuccin flavours shown side by side, from the pale Latte palette through the three darker flavours](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/catppuccin/catppuccin/main/assets/palette/demo.png)

*Figure 1. The four flavours, side by side. The hues keep their names across the family; only their values change.*

At a glance, the base surfaces run from Latte's ![Latte base swatch](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/catppuccin/catppuccin/main/assets/palette/circles/latte_base.png) soft off-white, through Frappé's ![Frappé base swatch](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/catppuccin/catppuccin/main/assets/palette/circles/frappe_base.png) muted slate and Macchiato's ![Macchiato base swatch](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/catppuccin/catppuccin/main/assets/palette/circles/macchiato_base.png) indigo grey, to Mocha's ![Mocha base swatch](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/catppuccin/catppuccin/main/assets/palette/circles/mocha_base.png) near-black violet. The little swatches are easy to underestimate. They are the foundation every other decision rests on.

The four flavours are not four independent themes. They are one theme with four sets of values. This distinction changes how you test. You do not need to re-review the layout in every flavour; you need to review the *relationships* — is `text` readable on `base`, is `surface0` distinct from `surface1`, does the primary button still stand out from a secondary button? If those relationships hold in the extremes (Latte and Mocha), the middle flavours almost always follow. We still check all four, because "almost always" is how bugs get a foothold, but the extreme-first approach catches most problems early.

&gt; [!TIP]
&gt; Start with Latte and Mocha, not with your favourite flavour. They are the furthest apart in luminance, so a palette decision that works at both ends has a much better chance of working in the middle.

One practical consequence: ~~hard-coded hex values~~ in component CSS became a thing we actively hunt for. A search for `#` in our component stylesheets is part of the release checklist, with a small allow-list for `transparent`, `currentColor`, and one shadow definition that is intentionally neutral. Everything else has to go through a token.

