## Wiring the switcher

A theme switcher is a small piece of code that attracts a surprising amount of complexity. There are four things it has to do: expose a control, remember the choice, respect the system preference when there is no choice, and avoid showing the wrong theme for a split second while the page loads. We'll take them in order.

### The markup

We put the control in the navigation bar as a simple group of buttons. The important part is the `data-set-theme` attribute; the script reads it and does one thing.

```html
<div class="btn-group btn-group-sm" role="group" aria-label="Choose a palette flavour">
  <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-primary" data-set-theme="system" aria-pressed="false">System</button>
  <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-primary" data-set-theme="latte" aria-pressed="false">Latte</button>
  <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-primary" data-set-theme="frappe" aria-pressed="false">Frappé</button>
  <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-primary" data-set-theme="macchiato" aria-pressed="false">Macchiato</button>
  <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-primary" data-set-theme="mocha" aria-pressed="false">Mocha</button>
</div>
```

Two details are easy to miss. First, `aria-pressed` is updated by the script, so the control announces its state to a screen reader rather than merely looking selected. Second, "System" is a real option, not a hidden default. People who prefer their operating system to decide should be able to get back to that behaviour after experimenting.

### The script

The script is intentionally tiny. It stores one string, resolves `system` through `matchMedia`, and writes the result to the root element.

```js
const root = document.documentElement;
const keys = document.querySelectorAll('[data-set-theme]');
const media = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');

function resolve(choice) {
  return choice === 'system'
    ? (media.matches ? 'mocha' : 'latte')
    : choice;
}

function apply(choice, persist = true) {
  const flavour = resolve(choice);
  root.dataset.theme = flavour;
  root.dataset.bsTheme = flavour === 'latte' ? 'light' : 'dark';

  keys.forEach((key) =&gt; {
    key.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(key.dataset.setTheme === choice));
  });

  if (persist) localStorage.setItem('ctp-theme', choice);
}

keys.forEach((key) =&gt; {
  key.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; apply(key.dataset.setTheme));
});

media.addEventListener('change', () =&gt; {
  if (localStorage.getItem('ctp-theme') === 'system') apply('system', false);
});

apply(localStorage.getItem('ctp-theme') ?? 'system', false);
```

Notice that we store the *choice*, not the resolved flavour. If a person selects "System" and later changes their operating system theme, our page follows. Storing `mocha` instead would freeze them in the past.

### The first-paint problem

The script above runs after the document has parsed, which means the browser may paint the default colours before the saved theme is applied. On a fast machine the flash lasts a frame. On a cold cache, in a browser with extensions, it can be long enough to be unpleasant — especially when the saved theme is dark and the default page is light.

The fix is a few lines in the ``, before any stylesheet can render:

```html

```

The `try` block is not paranoia. Storage access throws in private browsing modes and in embedded webviews with restrictive settings. A theme switcher should degrade to "default theme", not to "blank page".

&gt; A note on transitions: it is tempting to animate every colour change with `transition: all .3s`. Don't. On a full-page theme switch that repaints hundreds of elements, *all* includes layout-affecting properties you did not mean to animate. We transition `background-color`, `color`, and `border-color` on a short list of components, and nothing else.

