## Remapping Bootstrap tokens without fighting it

Bootstrap exposes a large set of `--bs-*` custom properties. Most of them are derived from a smaller set of base colours; the others are component-specific. We override the base set and let Bootstrap's own derivations do the rest wherever possible. The exceptions are the focus ring and the table stripes, both of which we handle explicitly.

```css
:root,
[data-theme='latte'],
[data-theme='frappe'],
[data-theme='macchiato'],
[data-theme='mocha'] {
  --bs-body-bg: var(--surface-page);
  --bs-body-color: var(--text-primary);
  --bs-border-color: var(--border-subtle);
  --bs-secondary-color: var(--text-muted);
  --bs-tertiary-bg: var(--surface-sunken);
  --bs-emphasis-color: var(--text-primary);

  --bs-primary: var(--ctp-blue);
  --bs-secondary: var(--ctp-surface2);
  --bs-success: var(--ctp-green);
  --bs-info: var(--ctp-sky);
  --bs-warning: var(--ctp-yellow);
  --bs-danger: var(--ctp-red);

  --bs-link-color: var(--ctp-blue);
  --bs-link-hover-color: var(--ctp-lavender);
  --bs-focus-ring-color: var(--state-focus);
}

[data-bs-theme='dark'] {
  color-scheme: dark;
}
```

The `color-scheme` declaration is small but important. It tells the browser to render form controls, scrollbars, and other native UI in a matching style. Without it, a carefully themed dark page can still show a bright white native date picker, which breaks the illusion instantly.

### Which tokens are worth overriding

Not every token deserves attention. Our rule is to override a token when its default value is visible *without the user asking for anything*. That gives us a short, defensible list:

- **Always override**
  - Body background and colour — these cover the entire viewport.
  - Link colour and its hover state — the primary navigation affordance.
  - Border colour — it defines every card, input, and table row.
  - Focus ring colour — a state that must be unmistakable.
  - Semantic colours: primary, success, danger, warning, info.
- **Override only when needed**
  - Placeholder colour, if the default is too faint in one flavour.
  - Table stripe background, because transparency over a tinted surface behaves differently in Latte than in Mocha.
  - Modal backdrop opacity, which we tune per flavour rather than globally.
- **Leave alone**
  - Spacing and sizing scales. They are structural, and our palette has nothing to say about them.
  - Breakpoints and z-index values. Changing these to "fix" a colour problem is a sign you are solving the wrong problem.
  - Transition timings, except where reduced motion requires it.

That last category grows more important over time. Every token you override is a token you own, and every token you own is a token you must re-check when upstream changes. We keep a count in our release notes; it is currently thirty-one, down from sixty-eight in the first version.

### When the cascade bites

The most frustrating bug we shipped was not a colour mistake. It was a specificity mistake: a component stylesheet loaded after our theme file, setting `--bs-body-bg` on a wrapper element. Because custom properties inherit, that wrapper and everything inside it used the old colour, while the rest of the page followed the switcher. The visual result looked like a broken gradient.

The fix was not `!important`. We moved token definitions into their own stylesheet and loaded it last, then scoped component overrides to classes that never set base tokens:

```diff
- .dashboard-panel { --bs-body-bg: #ffffff; }
+ .dashboard-panel { background: var(--bs-body-bg); }
```

The diff illustrates the general rule: components should *consume* tokens, never redefine the shared ones. A component may define a private token of its own — `--panel-accent`, for instance — but the moment it says `--bs-body-bg`, it has quietly opted out of the theme system.

There is one legitimate use of `!important` in our stylesheet, and we comment it every time someone asks. Third-party widgets occasionally ship hard-coded colours with high specificity. We scope the override to the widget's container and document which version of the widget requires it. When that widget is upgraded, the override is the first thing we delete.

