## Components after the swap

Palette work only matters at the component level. This section walks through the pieces we touch most often and the details that surprised us.

### Buttons and focus

Primary buttons use a saturated accent — blue by default, mauve for the "create" action in our product, red for destructive operations. Secondary buttons use a surface colour with a subtle border. The difference should be readable in greyscale, which is a quick way to catch an accent choice that is really just a decoration choice.

```css
.btn-primary {
  --bs-btn-bg: var(--bs-primary);
  --bs-btn-border-color: var(--bs-primary);
  --bs-btn-hover-bg: var(--ctp-lavender);
  --bs-btn-hover-border-color: var(--ctp-lavender);
  --bs-btn-active-bg: var(--ctp-sapphire);
  --bs-btn-disabled-bg: var(--ctp-surface2);
  --bs-btn-disabled-border-color: transparent;
}
```

The focus ring deserves a paragraph of its own. Bootstrap's default ring uses a translucent version of the component colour. On a dark background, translucent blue can land closer to the background than the button itself, which makes keyboard navigation feel uncertain. We use a solid mauve ring with a two-pixel offset, and verify it against every surface the button can appear on: page, card, modal, and table header.

&gt; Reviewer's note, preserved because it is still true: "If you can find the focus ring only by squinting, it is not a focus ring. It is a rumour."

### Forms

Form controls are where `color-scheme` earns its keep. Text inputs, selects, checkboxes, and the native date picker all render differently depending on it. We also set an explicit `background-image` for the validation icons only when we can match their colour to the semantic token; otherwise we let Bootstrap's own icons stand, because a half-matched icon looks worse than a consistent default.

```html
<div class="mb-3">
  <label for="email" class="form-label">Email address</label>
  <input type="email" class="form-control" id="email" placeholder="you@example.com" aria-describedby="emailHelp">
  <div id="emailHelp" class="form-text">
    We'll only use this to send build notifications.
  </div>
</div>
<div class="form-check mb-3">
  <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="subscribe">
  <label class="form-check-label" for="subscribe">
    Send me the monthly changelog
  </label>
</div>
```

Helper text is a quiet element that often gets too loud. We place it on `subtext1`, not on `overlay1`, and give it a size step down from the label. The information is secondary; the colour should say so without making it unreadable. In Latte especially, the difference between `subtext0` and `overlay2` is small enough that we avoid using both on the same screen.

### Tables and alerts

Tables carry the most surface area of any component, which makes them the fastest way to expose a weak palette. Stripes, hover states, and borders all have to work together. We use `surface0` for the stripe and `surface1` on hover, with `surface2` reserved for borders. The header row gets a slightly darker background and medium-weight text, but no accent colour — a coloured header tends to pull the eye away from the data.

| State | Background | Text | Border | Timing |
|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|
| Resting | `base` | `text` | `surface2` | — |
| Hover | `surface0` | `text` | `surface2` | 120ms |
| Selected | `surface1` | `text` | `blue` | immediate |
| Disabled | `mantle` | `overlay1` | `surface1` | — |
| Error row | `red` at 12% | `red` | `red` at 40% | immediate |

Alerts follow a similar discipline. Each semantic alert uses its colour for the border and icon, but keeps the background close to the page surface. A full-saturation red panel is appropriate for a destructive confirmation, not for a form that rejected an email address. The severity of the colour should match the severity of the event.

```html
<div class="alert alert-warning d-flex align-items-center" role="alert">
  <span class="badge rounded-pill text-bg-warning me-3">Heads up</span>
  <div>
    The build finished with <strong>three warnings</strong>.
    Nothing failed, but the output changed.
  </div>
</div>
```

### Modals and layered surfaces

Modals reveal whether your surface hierarchy is real. A modal backdrop dims the page; the modal itself must read as *above* it. In dark flavours we use `mantle` for the modal background and `crust` for the page beneath, so the layer order follows the palette's natural progression. In Latte we invert the logic: the page is `base`, the modal is `base` again with a stronger shadow, and the backdrop uses a neutral translucent overlay. Trying to use the same layering rule in both directions was our first significant mistake.

- What worked in the end
  - One rule for dark flavours: each layer is *lighter* than the one below it.
  - One rule for Latte: each layer is *whiter* than the one below it, reinforced by shadow.
  - The backdrop is never coloured with an accent. Its job is to recede.
- What did not work
  - A translucent mauve backdrop. It looked clever and made every modal feel like a celebration.
  - A coloured modal header. It competed with the alert inside the modal and confused the hierarchy.
  - Animating the backdrop separately from the dialog. The two arrived at different times and felt broken.

### A review checklist that actually gets used

We keep this list beside the pull request template. The checked items are the ones that have caught real regressions; the unchecked ones are reminders for the next release, not signs of unfinished work.

- [x] Check the page in Latte and Mocha, not just the default flavour.
- [x] Tab through every interactive element and confirm the focus ring is visible.
- [x] Search changed stylesheets for `#` and justify every remaining hex value.
- [x] Confirm `color-scheme` matches the selected flavour.
- [ ] Capture updated screenshots for the documentation site.
- [ ] Re-run the keyboard pass after the next Bootstrap minor upgrade.
- [ ] Ask someone who has not seen the change to find the primary action in under three seconds.

That last item is deliberately subjective. We have caught more hierarchy problems with that one informal test than with any automated check, because it measures what the interface *communicates* rather than what it contains.

