## Accessibility is not a palette decision alone

It is tempting to treat accessibility as a contrast calculation you perform once and file away. Contrast is necessary, but it is only the beginning. A theme also has to survive keyboard navigation, screen readers, reduced-motion preferences, forced-colour modes, and the simple fact that people use interfaces in environments you cannot control.

### Contrast as a relationship, not a number

We measure contrast between *pairs* that actually occur in the product: text on page, text on card, muted text on stripe, button label on button background, focus ring on every surface it can appear against. Measuring colours in isolation tells you very little. `subtext1` may have excellent contrast on `base` and fail completely on `surface2`.

| Pair | Latte | Macchiato | Mocha | Target |
|:---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| `text` on `base` | 12.1:1 | 13.4:1 | 13.6:1 | ≥ 7:1 |
| `subtext1` on `base` | 7.4:1 | 10.3:1 | 10.6:1 | ≥ 7:1 |
| `text` on `surface0` | 10.6:1 | 11.2:1 | 11.5:1 | ≥ 7:1 |
| `blue` on `base` | 5.2:1 | 5.7:1 | 6.1:1 | ≥ 4.5:1 |
| focus ring on `mantle` | 4.8:1 | 5.1:1 | 5.4:1 | ≥ 3:1 |

These figures are from our own measurements of the flavour values in use; your rendered values will depend on the exact tokens you choose. The target column follows [WCAG 2.2][wcag] guidance: 4.5:1 for ordinary text, 7:1 for enhanced contrast, and 3:1 for non-text elements such as focus indicators[^motion]. We aim for the enhanced threshold on body copy because pastel palettes give us enough room to do so without sacrificing their character.

### Motion and the people who don't want it

A theme switch is animation-adjacent work. Even if you do not add a dedicated transition, the browser may animate native controls. Respecting `prefers-reduced-motion` is a few lines of CSS and one of the highest-value accessibility changes you can make.

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}
```

The `!important` declarations here are intentional and standard practice for this particular override: a user preference should win over component-level animation settings. This is the one case where the earlier "no `!important`" rule has an explicit exception, and the comment in the stylesheet says so.

### Testing with a keyboard, not a mouse

Automated contrast checks are useful, but they cannot tell you whether a focus order is sensible. Our manual pass follows a fixed sequence:

1. Load the page with no saved theme, so the system preference decides the starting flavour.
2. Press <kbd>Tab</kbd> from the very top and follow the ring through the navigation, including the flavour buttons.
3. Open a modal with the keyboard, tab to the last control, and confirm focus does not escape behind the backdrop.
4. Switch flavour *while the modal is open* and confirm the modal updates along with the page.
5. Close the modal with <kbd>Escape</kbd> and confirm focus returns to the trigger.
6. Repeat with `prefers-reduced-motion` enabled at the operating-system level.

Step four is the one we added after a bug report. Our first switcher updated `document.documentElement` but left the modal's portal outside the styled tree, so the dialog kept the old colours while the rest of the page changed. It looked like a rendering glitch and was, in fact, a scope problem.

