## A small refactor, start to finish

Theory is cheap. This section documents an actual afternoon of work: taking one component from hard-coded Bootstrap defaults to a flavour-aware implementation. The component is a simple "build status" card with a title, a badge, and a progress element. Nothing about it is exotic, which is why it is a good example.

### The starting point

The original markup was fine. The CSS was where the trouble lived.

```css
.status-card {
  background: #ffffff;
  color: #212529;
  border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
  border-radius: .5rem;
}

.status-card .badge {
  background: #0d6efd;
}

.status-card .progress-bar {
  background: #198754;
}
```

Three hard-coded neutral colours, two hard-coded accents, and no relationship to the rest of the page. In Latte it looked acceptable by accident. In Mocha it was a bright white rectangle floating in a dark interface.

### Step 1: name the intent

Before touching a value, we write down what each colour is *for*. This takes five minutes and regularly saves an hour of guesswork later.

| Old value | Intent | Token |
|:---|:---|:---|
| `#ffffff` | Raised surface | `--surface-raised` |
| `#212529` | Primary text | `--text-primary` |
| `#dee2e6` | Subtle border | `--border-subtle` |
| `#0d6efd` | Informational badge | `--bs-info` |
| `#198754` | Progress / success | `--bs-success` |

The table is not documentation for its own sake. It is the diff review: a reviewer can check the mapping without opening a colour picker.

### Step 2: put the flavour behind a variable

Now we rewrite the CSS using only tokens. The component no longer knows which flavour is active, and it no longer needs to.

```css
.status-card {
  background: var(--surface-raised);
  color: var(--text-primary);
  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  border-radius: var(--bs-border-radius);
}

.status-card .badge {
  background: var(--bs-info);
  color: var(--ctp-crust);
}

.status-card .progress-bar {
  background: var(--bs-success);
}
```

Note the badge text colour. A saturated `sky` badge works with a dark label in every flavour, because `crust` is dark in Latte and darker still in the other three. Choosing `white` instead would have failed in Latte, where `sky` is a light blue. This is the kind of relationship that only appears when you check both extremes.

### Step 3: verify in all four flavours

The verification is scripted, but the final look is not. We generate screenshots at a fixed viewport in each flavour and compare them side by side.

```bash
for flavour in latte frappe macchiato mocha; do
  CTP_FLAVOR="$flavour" npm run storybook:screenshot -- --component=status-card
done
npm run a11y:check -- --pairs=status-card
```

The first command produces four images. The second checks the pairs we listed earlier, with the component's actual rendered backgrounds. Both have to pass before the pull request is reviewed. When a human finally looks at the four images, they are checking something no script can measure: does the card still feel like it belongs to the same product?

---

## Pitfalls I keep running into

Every long-lived theming project accumulates a small museum of mistakes. These are ours, offered in the hope that yours will be shorter.

- **Translucent black as a universal shade.** `rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)` looks neutral until you place it over a violet-tinted dark surface, where it reads as muddy brown. Use the palette's own surface steps instead.
- **The "one more border" reflex.** When two surfaces are too similar, the first instinct is often to add a border. Sometimes the right fix is to pick surfaces that are further apart. Adding borders everywhere produces an interface made of boxes.
- **Semantic colours used for decoration.** A green dot meaning "online" is useful. A green left border on every card because it looks fresh is not. Once a colour has a meaning, spending it on nothing dilutes it.
  - Related: a yellow warning badge on a yellow informational callout. Two yellows with different meanings on one screen is one yellow too many.
- **Forgetting disabled states.** Disabled controls need to be clearly inactive but not invisible. `overlay1` on `surface1` works in dark flavours; in Latte we use `subtext0` on `surface2` and accept the slightly stronger appearance.
- **Assuming the system preference never changes.** People switch their operating system theme at night. If your switcher is set to "System", it must listen for that change.
  - And if it is *not* set to "System", it must not follow the change. These are two different bugs and we have shipped both.
- **Testing only on the default flavour.** A theme that works in Macchiato is a Macchiato theme. It is not yet a product theme.
- **Letting a component redefine a shared token.** One component doing this is a curiosity. Three doing it is a system whose behaviour depends on stylesheet load order.
- **Treating documentation as a chore.** When a token is undocumented, someone will invent a local replacement. We have found three different "muted text" colours in a single codebase, each created by a person who could not find the real one.

The pattern behind most of these is the same: a local fix that solves today's screenshot while weakening tomorrow's system. The discipline is not harder than the shortcut; it just asks you to name what you are doing before you do it.

---

## Keeping the theme alive for years

The hardest part of a theming project is the part after launch. Bootstrap releases minor versions. Catppuccin refines a hue. A new component arrives with its own opinion about borders. Without a maintenance rhythm, the theme slowly drifts back towards the framework defaults, and nobody notices until someone compares a screenshot from two years ago.

Our release checklist is deliberately short enough that people actually run it:

- [x] Regenerate tokens from the upstream palette and review the diff.
- [x] Run the contrast-pair check across all four flavours.
- [x] Tab through the main navigation and one modal in each flavour.
- [x] Search for hard-coded colours in changed files.
- [x] Confirm the first-paint script still runs before the stylesheet.
- [ ] Update the documentation screenshots when a visual token changes.
- [ ] Review the list of `!important` exceptions and remove any that are no longer needed.

&gt; The goal is not to freeze the design. It is to make change legible. If you can explain, in one sentence, why every colour in a component is what it is, the theme is healthy. If you cannot, it is already drifting — you just have not measured it yet.

### Documentation as a repair tool

We keep three documents next to the code: a token table, a decision log, and a short page of "known exceptions". The token table maps intent names to flavour values. The decision log records why we chose a token, including the options we rejected. The exceptions page lists the handful of places where we knowingly break a rule, with the condition under which the exception may be removed.

The decision log has been the most useful of the three. It turns arguments that happened once into answers that remain available. When someone asks why the focus ring is mauve rather than blue, the log says: blue is the primary action colour, and using it for focus would blur the distinction between "you can click this" and "the keyboard is here". Nobody has to re-litigate that choice, and more importantly, nobody has to guess whether it was deliberate.

Further reading we return to often:

- The [Bootstrap CSS variables reference][bs-vars], for the authoritative list of `--bs-*` tokens.
- The project's home at <https: catppuccin.com="">, and the [palette page][palette-site] in particular, for flavour values and usage guidance.
- The [WCAG 2.2 contrast guidance][wcag], for the numbers behind our accessibility targets.
- The [MDN guide to `prefers-color-scheme`][mdn-scheme], for the details of system preference handling.
- Our own [token generation script][tokens-repo], which is small enough to read in one sitting[^footnote].

---

## Closing: the point of quiet

A quiet interface is not the absence of design. It is design that has done its arguing in advance, so that the person using the product does not have to do it in real time. Every token we named, every contrast pair we measured, and every keyboard pass we ran was a small argument settled before it could become a question in someone else's head.

The result in this case is unremarkable in the best possible way. The navigation collapses when it should. The modal opens, traps focus, and closes with <kbd>Escape</kbd>. The buttons read clearly, the tables stay calm, and the four flavours feel like the same product seen under different lighting. Nothing about the interface asks to be admired. That is the compliment we were aiming for.

If you take one thing from this article, let it be the ordering: name the intent, then attach a value, then let the framework consume it. Reversing that order produces a stylesheet that works today and resists you tomorrow. Keeping it produces a theme that can absorb four palettes, two redesigns, and one very opinionated Friday code review without falling apart.

